 HotSpot Java Virtual Machine (JVM), which is part of the Java Development Kit (JDK). Specially crafted Base64 input could crash the JVM and allow for unauthenticated remote code execution. Actions to take CISA urges organizations to take the following actions: Identify affected Java installations. Review your software inventory for JDKs and Java runtimes, i</description><chr2:heatScore>67.5</chr2:heatScore></item><item><title>Critical Java4Shell flaw allows RCE attacks on major Java applications</title><link>https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/critical-java4shell-flaw-allows-rce-attacks-on-major-java-applications/</link><guid isPermaLink="false">urn:sha256:fca3dc2fb77689c74c126f3134f3c8bb6edce29d1c049c2bb1353a2e03b9b0d2</guid><category>NEWS</category><chr2:sourceName>BleepingComputer</chr2:sourceName><chr2:firstObservedAtJST>2026-11-18T08:22:11.682252+09:00</chr2:firstObservedAtJST><pubDate>Tue, 17 Nov 2026 23:04:21 GMT</pubDate><description>A critical vulnerability dubbed Java4Shell in OpenJDK, the open-source implementation of Java, can allow unauthenticated attackers to remotely execute code on popular enterprise applications, including Jenkins, Atlassian Jira, Confluence, and Keycloak. [...]</description><chr2:heatScore>65.0</chr2:heatScore></item></channel></rss>